<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perpetual Maritime Truce</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1853</span> · Also known as Treaty of Maritime Peace in Perpetuity</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Perpetual Maritime Truce was an agreement of 1853 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the rulers of the coast now forming the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, forbidding all hostilities at sea between them. It made permanent a series of seasonal truces begun in 1835 and gave Britain the role of enforcing peace at sea. The coast became known as the Trucial Coast and its sheikhdoms as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trucial-states">Trucial States</a>.</p></div>
